candidates.description:
Root cause: JakartaMultiPartRequest builds its upload-error message by concatenating an exception-class-derived key and passing the exception's own message as the fallback string to LocalizedTextUtil.findText:

  protected String buildErrorMessage(Throwable e, Object[] args) {
      String errorKey = "struts.messages.upload.error." + e.getClass().getSimpleName();
      ...
      return LocalizedTextUtil.findText(this.getClass(), errorKey, defaultLocale, e.getMessage(), args);
  }

This method is invoked from parse(), whose catch block handles any Throwable/FileUploadException raised while parsing attacker supplied multipart input (parse():89-110 -> catch (Exception e) at 101 -> buildErrorMessage at 105).

Sink: LocalizedTextUtil.findText(Class,String,Locale,String,Object[]) (line 360) obtains the current ActionContext ValueStack and delegates to the 6-arg overload (line 408). When the bundle key is not found (the generated key like "struts.messages.upload.error.InvalidContentTypeException" is not present in a resource bundle for this class), control reaches getDefaultMessage(...) (lines 532-541 -> 648-671). There, if findDefaultText(key, locale) returns null, message is set to the supplied defaultMessage (line 657) and is then evaluated:

  MessageFormat mf = buildMessageFormat(TextParseUtil.translateVariables(message, valueStack), locale);  // line 663

TextParseUtil.translateVariables(...) (lines 47-49 -> 125-127 -> 153-167) constructs an OGNL evaluator whose evaluate() calls stack.findValue(parsedValue, asType) (line 157) for each %{...} / ${...} segment found by the TextParser.

Security impact: Commons FileUpload exceptions raised for malformed multipart requests include the request's Content-Type value verbatim in their getMessage() (e.g. InvalidContentTypeException). A crafted Content-Type header containing %{...} expression syntax therefore flows into the OGNL interpreter, enabling expression evaluation with the action's ValueStack (the documented multipart-parser RCE pattern). Because the flow originates in the dispatcher's multipart wrapping rather than in request parameters, it bypasses the parameters interceptor's OGNL/param-name restrictions.

Related observable-but-secondary sink: the same evaluate() result is also used to construct a MessageFormat whose pattern is the attacker influenced text (buildMessageFormat, lines 700-711), so malformed pattern text can also trigger a runtime exception; and messageFormats is a shared static cache keyed on (pattern, locale) (lines 856-887), so attacker unique patterns accumulate there (minor memory growth), but this is not the primary concern.
